Yes, exactly. Every single software vulnerability on earth follows precise definition frameworks and strict, predictable technical rules.
In cybersecurity, hacking isn't magic or random guesswork. It is a science of rules. Vulnerabilities only exist because computers are rigid machines that execute instructions exactly as programmed. When a developer accidentally sets up a flawed rule, an attacker uses the laws of computer science to exploit it.
Here is how the entire cybersecurity industry categorizes, defines, and maps the rules for every vulnerability on earth.
------------------------------
## 1. 📖 The Universal Definitions: CWE & CVE
To make sure every engineer on earth speaks the same language, the cybersecurity industry uses two massive, standardized dictionary databases to define flaws:

* CWE (Common Weakness Enumeration): This is the dictionary of the vulnerability type itself (the root cause) [mitre.org]. For example, SQL Injection is defined globally as CWE-89 [mitre.org], and Path Traversal is CWE-22 [mitre.org].
* CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures): This defines a specific, real-world instance of a flaw found in a real piece of software [cve.org]. For example, if a researcher finds a path traversal bug in a specific version of WordPress, it gets assigned a unique tracker id like CVE-2026-XXXX.

